#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Fake `claude` CLI for hermetic tests — never invokes the real binary.

Reads FAKE_CLAUDE_MODE from the environment to decide which canned
--output-format json payload to print; ignores argv beyond accepting whatever
-p/--output-format/--permission-mode/--setting-sources/--model flags the
harness passes. FAKE_CLAUDE_SLEEP (seconds, default 5) controls the "timeout"
mode's sleep duration.

Note: the harness invokes CLAUDE_BIN identically for both the agent call and
the judge call, so a single script must serve both roles. The dedicated
run_agent tests use mode "agent" (plain prose result) and the dedicated
judge.score tests use "judge"/"judge_fenced"/"judge_no_overall" (JSON
result). The end-to-end orchestration test (single CLAUDE_BIN/env for the
whole `main()` invocation) instead uses "agent_and_judge", whose result is a
JSON string of judge-shaped dimensions/overall — that string is a perfectly
valid transcript for the agent call, and it's exactly what judge.score's
parser expects for the judge call, so one canned response satisfies both
subprocess invocations inside a single run.

Mode "agent_env" replies with a JSON snapshot of the probe variables the
harness's agent_env() is expected to have set (SKILLS_EVALS_PROBE, PATH).

Mode "echo_prompt" reports how the prompt reached it — stdin byte count and
digest, and the argv it was handed — so a test can prove the judge prompt
travels on stdin rather than in an argument vector Linux caps at 128 KB.

Eight modes serve the pairwise judge (skills-evals#81): "judge_pairwise"
returns a canned ranking of three blind draft labels plus per-draft
dimension scores; "judge_pairwise_incomplete" drops a label from the
ranking and "judge_pairwise_unknown_label" invents one;
"judge_pairwise_sloppy_labels" answers in lower case with stray padding
(the labels are still the ones it was given, so the scorer must normalise
rather than reject); and "judge_pairwise_score_out_of_range" /
"judge_pairwise_score_nan" / "judge_pairwise_score_huge" put an 11, a NaN
and a 10**400 on the 0-10 scale, so the scorer's validation has something
to reject (the last has no float, so testing it with math.isfinite raises
rather than answering); and "judge_pairwise_duplicate_labels" scores one
draft twice, once in each case, which label normalisation used to collapse
into a single entry. The canned ranking is fixed while the
LABEL each draft carries is shuffled per trial by the scorer, which is what
lets a test read the shuffle back out of the resulting rank.

Every canned JSON result carries a `modelUsage` object keyed by the
`--model` it was invoked with (else "fake-default-model"), as the real CLI's
result does; run_eval.py records its keys as `models_used` (#202).

$FAKE_CLAUDE_RATE_LIMIT_FILE, when set, names a JSON file holding a list of
batches, each a list of `rate_limit_info` objects. A call made with
`--verbose` then prints the real CLI's verbose shape instead of the single
result object: a message array of an init message, an assistant message
with a fixed timestamp, one `rate_limit_event` per object in the first batch,
and the result. That call takes its batch off the file, so successive calls
can report a moving account meter (#370); with no batch left the array
carries no event. A call without `--verbose` prints the single object and
takes nothing (the harness's own agent, judge and guard calls all carry it).

$FAKE_CLAUDE_FULL_USAGE, when set, gives every result `num_turns`, a
`total_cost_usd`, `usage.cache_creation` and a complete, costed `modelUsage`
(#371); without it the output is unchanged.

`--version` is handled unconditionally (regardless of FAKE_CLAUDE_MODE) so
that harness/run_canary.py's `claude --version` recording call succeeds even
when a mode simulates a failure.

Three modes serve the guidance subject's per-arm delivery guard (#97):
  - guidance_probe_literal: guidance_probe, but it answers the QUESTION IT WAS
    ASKED rather than always reporting everything — every magic word when the
    prompt asks for every one of them, otherwise the first one it finds. It
    exists because the suite must measure the production prompt and not a
    behaviour that prompt never requests: with a decoy in context a
    contaminated control carries TWO magic words, and a probe obeying a
    SINGULAR "reply with that magic word" can report the decoy alone and leave
    the treatment token hidden. Measured that way, the contaminated control
    scored clean.
  - guidance_probe: loads "memory" the way the real CLI would —
    $CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR/CLAUDE.md when --setting-sources names `user`,
    ./CLAUDE.md when it names `project` — and replies with the magic word it
    finds there, or NO-MAGIC-WORD. $FAKE_CLAUDE_AMBIENT_MEMORY, when set,
    names a file loaded REGARDLESS of the config dir: that is the contaminated
    real ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md the guard exists to catch.
  - guidance_blind: never sees any memory, simulating an arm whose delivery
    silently failed.

$FAKE_CLAUDE_ARGV_LOG, when set, gets one JSON line per invocation
({argv, env_keys, cwd}) in EVERY mode, so a test can assert which
--setting-sources an arm was invoked with and that the environment allowlist
scrubbed what it claims to.

Three modes simulate harness/run_canary.py's guidance-bridge canary:
  - canary_loader: mimics Claude Code's real memory loader — expands an
    `@AGENTS.md`-style import line in ./CLAUDE.md (outside fenced code
    blocks) and replies with CLAUDE.md's content plus whatever it imported.
    With no CLAUDE.md it replies with AGENTS.md's content (native AGENTS.md
    support, 2.1.277), and with neither it replies NO-MAGIC-WORD.
  - canary_blind: always replies "NO-MAGIC-WORD", simulating a regressed
    loader that injects nothing.
  - canary_forager: replies with the concatenation of every *.md file in cwd
    (sorted), simulating either read-tool foraging or native AGENTS.md
    support — everything is visible regardless of layout.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import hashlib
import json
import os
import re
import sys
import time
from pathlib import Path

MODE = os.environ.get("FAKE_CLAUDE_MODE", "agent")

USAGE = {
    "input_tokens": 1234,
    "output_tokens": 567,
    "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0,
    "cache_read_input_tokens": 0,
}

JUDGE_DIMENSIONS = [
    {"name": "Completeness", "score": 8, "rationale": "Filtered every workflow found."},
    {"name": "Correctness", "score": 9, "rationale": "Required check still always fires."},
    {"name": "Restraint", "score": 7, "rationale": "Left schedule-only workflows alone."},
    {"name": "Communication", "score": 6, "rationale": "Explained changes briefly."},
]


# Pairwise (#81): three drafts, labelled A/B/C by the scorer after it has
# shuffled them. B is ranked best, then A, then C — deliberately NOT the
# label order, so a scorer that ignored the ranking and took the first label
# would score differently.
PAIRWISE_RANKING = ["B", "A", "C"]

PAIRWISE_DIMENSIONS_BY_LABEL = {
    "A": [{"name": "specificity", "score": 7,
           "rationale": "Names the requisition number."},
          {"name": "register match", "score": 6,
           "rationale": "Warm, plain sign-off."},
          {"name": "absence of corporate filler", "score": 8,
           "rationale": "No buzzwords."}],
    "B": [{"name": "specificity", "score": 9,
           "rationale": "Both facts, quoted exactly."},
          {"name": "register match", "score": 9,
           "rationale": "Greets by name and hedges."},
          {"name": "absence of corporate filler", "score": 9,
           "rationale": "Nothing to strip."}],
    "C": [{"name": "specificity", "score": 3,
           "rationale": "Vague about the timing."},
          {"name": "register match", "score": 4,
           "rationale": "Reads like a form letter."},
          {"name": "absence of corporate filler", "score": 2,
           "rationale": "Buzzwords throughout."}],
}


# The model a canned result says served it, when no `--model` was passed. The
# real CLI's `--output-format json` result carries `modelUsage` keyed by the
# model id(s) that served the turns; run_eval.py records those keys as an
# arm's `models_used` (#202). With `--model X` the fake reports X, which is
# what the real CLI does for a model it can serve.
DEFAULT_FAKE_MODEL = "fake-default-model"


#: The timestamp on the assistant message of a verbose array: fixed, so a
#: test can name the time a meter event is stamped with.
MESSAGE_TIMESTAMP = "2026-10-10T15:10:13.663Z"


def _next_meter_batch() -> list | None:
    """This call's `rate_limit_info` objects, taken off
    $FAKE_CLAUDE_RATE_LIMIT_FILE; None when the call prints no array."""
    path = os.environ.get("FAKE_CLAUDE_RATE_LIMIT_FILE")
    if not path or "--verbose" not in sys.argv:
        return None
    batches = json.loads(Path(path).read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
    Path(path).write_text(json.dumps(batches[1:]), encoding="utf-8")
    return batches[0] if batches else []


def emit(payload: dict) -> None:
    payload.setdefault("modelUsage", {
        (_flag_value("--model") or DEFAULT_FAKE_MODEL): {
            "inputTokens": USAGE["input_tokens"],
            "outputTokens": USAGE["output_tokens"]}})
    if os.environ.get("FAKE_CLAUDE_FULL_USAGE"):
        # Every figure the harness records per role (#371), on every result:
        # turns, a cost, the cache-write split and a complete `modelUsage`.
        payload.update({"num_turns": 2, "total_cost_usd": 0.07,
                        "usage": {**USAGE, "cache_creation": {
                            "ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 3,
                            "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 4}}})
        for entry in payload["modelUsage"].values():
            entry.update({"cacheReadInputTokens": 5,
                          "cacheCreationInputTokens": 7, "costUSD": 0.07,
                          "costBasis": "list"})
    batch = _next_meter_batch()
    if batch is None:
        print(json.dumps(payload))
        return
    print(json.dumps([
        {"type": "system", "subtype": "init", "session_id": "fake-session"},
        {"type": "assistant", "timestamp": MESSAGE_TIMESTAMP,
         "message": {"content": [{"type": "text",
                                  "text": payload.get("result")}]}},
        *({"type": "rate_limit_event", "rate_limit_info": info}
          for info in batch),
        {"type": "result", **payload}]))


def _load_claude_md() -> str:
    """canary_loader: expand `@file` import lines outside fenced code blocks."""
    claude_md = Path("CLAUDE.md")
    if not claude_md.is_file():
        # Native AGENTS.md support as Claude Code 2.1.277's release note
        # describes it: "in a project with no CLAUDE.md, Claude Code reads
        # AGENTS.md instead". Modeled from that note, not measured against a
        # real CLI (skills-evals#191). With a CLAUDE.md present AGENTS.md is
        # NOT read, which is why no-bridge and fence stay invisible.
        agents_md = Path("AGENTS.md")
        if agents_md.is_file():
            return agents_md.read_text(encoding="utf-8")
        return "NO-MAGIC-WORD"
    content = claude_md.read_text(encoding="utf-8")
    imported = []
    in_fence = False
    for line in content.splitlines():
        if line.startswith("```"):
            in_fence = not in_fence
            continue
        if not in_fence and line.startswith("@"):
            tokens = line[1:].split()
            if not tokens:
                continue
            imported_path = Path(tokens[0])
            if imported_path.is_file():
                imported.append(imported_path.read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
    return content + "".join(imported)


MAGIC_RE = re.compile(r"The magic word is (\S+?)\.")

# The wording harness/guidance.py's GUARD_PROMPT uses to ask for EVERY magic
# word rather than one. `guidance_probe_literal` keys off exactly this, and
# test/issues/test_issue_97.py reads the constant back out of this file with
# `ast` and asserts GUARD_PROMPT still carries it — so the prompt and this
# fake cannot drift apart and leave the suite measuring an arity the
# production prompt never asks for.
PLURAL_MARKER = "every one of them"


def _flag_value(name: str) -> str:
    """The value of `--name VALUE` in argv, or "" — the fake CLI has to READ
    --setting-sources to model memory loading, which is the whole point of the
    guidance guard."""
    if name in sys.argv:
        index = sys.argv.index(name)
        if index + 1 < len(sys.argv):
            return sys.argv[index + 1]
    return ""


def _read_if_file(path: Path) -> str:
    try:
        return path.read_text(encoding="utf-8")
    except OSError:
        return ""


def _loaded_memory() -> str:
    """What this invocation would have in context, per --setting-sources."""
    sources = _flag_value("--setting-sources").split(",")
    memory = ""
    if "user" in sources:
        config_dir = os.environ.get("CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR")
        if config_dir:
            memory += _read_if_file(Path(config_dir) / "CLAUDE.md")
    if "project" in sources:
        memory += _read_if_file(Path.cwd() / "CLAUDE.md")
    ambient = os.environ.get("FAKE_CLAUDE_AMBIENT_MEMORY")
    if ambient:
        # The contamination case: a real ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md that already IS
        # the guidance, reaching an arm that was never delivered to.
        memory += _read_if_file(Path(ambient))
    return memory


def _asked_for_every_word() -> bool:
    """Does the `-p` prompt this invocation was given ask for EVERY magic word?

    Whitespace-folded, because a fixture's YAML folded scalar and the Python
    constant wrap differently and mean the same thing.
    """
    return PLURAL_MARKER in " ".join(_flag_value("-p").split())


def _log_argv() -> None:
    log = os.environ.get("FAKE_CLAUDE_ARGV_LOG")
    if not log:
        return
    record = {"argv": sys.argv[1:], "env_keys": sorted(os.environ),
              "cwd": os.getcwd()}
    with open(log, "a", encoding="utf-8") as handle:
        handle.write(json.dumps(record) + "\n")


def main() -> int:
    _log_argv()
    if "--version" in sys.argv:
        print("fake-claude 0.0.0 (hermetic test stub)")
        return 0

    if MODE == "error":
        print("fake-claude: simulated CLI failure", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1

    if MODE == "timeout":
        time.sleep(float(os.environ.get("FAKE_CLAUDE_SLEEP", "5")))
        emit({"result": "late", "is_error": False, "usage": USAGE,
             "total_cost_usd": 0.01, "num_turns": 1, "duration_ms": 100,
             "session_id": "fake-session"})
        return 0

    if MODE == "agent_error":
        emit({
            "result": "Failed to audit the workflows: could not read .github/workflows.",
            "is_error": True,
            "usage": USAGE,
            "total_cost_usd": 0.02,
            "num_turns": 2,
            "duration_ms": 4200,
            "session_id": "fake-session",
        })
        return 0

    if MODE in ("judge", "judge_fenced", "judge_no_overall"):
        judge_json: dict = {"dimensions": JUDGE_DIMENSIONS}
        if MODE != "judge_no_overall":
            judge_json["overall"] = 7.5
        judge_text = json.dumps(judge_json)
        if MODE == "judge_fenced":
            judge_text = "```json\n" + judge_text + "\n```"
        emit({
            "result": judge_text,
            "is_error": False,
            "usage": USAGE,
            "total_cost_usd": 0.03,
            "num_turns": 1,
            "duration_ms": 800,
            "session_id": "fake-session",
        })
        return 0

    if MODE in ("judge_pairwise", "judge_pairwise_incomplete",
                "judge_pairwise_unknown_label", "judge_pairwise_sloppy_labels",
                "judge_pairwise_score_out_of_range", "judge_pairwise_score_nan",
                "judge_pairwise_score_huge", "judge_pairwise_duplicate_labels"):
        ranking = list(PAIRWISE_RANKING)
        dimensions = PAIRWISE_DIMENSIONS_BY_LABEL
        if MODE == "judge_pairwise_incomplete":
            ranking = ranking[:-1]
        elif MODE == "judge_pairwise_unknown_label":
            ranking = ranking[:-1] + ["Z"]
        elif MODE == "judge_pairwise_sloppy_labels":
            # A judge answering in lower case with stray padding. The labels
            # are still exactly the ones it was handed.
            ranking = [f" {label.lower()} " for label in ranking]
            dimensions = {label.lower(): dims
                          for label, dims in dimensions.items()}
        elif MODE in ("judge_pairwise_score_out_of_range",
                      "judge_pairwise_score_nan", "judge_pairwise_score_huge"):
            # 10**400 is the one that is not merely out of range: it has no
            # float, so math.isfinite() on it raises OverflowError instead
            # of answering.
            bad = {"judge_pairwise_score_out_of_range": 11,
                   "judge_pairwise_score_nan": float("nan"),
                   "judge_pairwise_score_huge": 10 ** 400}[MODE]
            dimensions = {label: [dict(dims[0], score=bad), *dims[1:]]
                          for label, dims in dimensions.items()}
        elif MODE == "judge_pairwise_duplicate_labels":
            # A judge that scores the same draft twice, once in each case.
            # Normalising the labels collapsed the two into one entry with
            # the last write winning, silently.
            dimensions = dict(dimensions,
                              a=[dict(d, score=0) for d in dimensions["A"]])
        emit({
            "result": json.dumps({"ranking": ranking,
                                  "dimensions": dimensions}),
            "is_error": False,
            "usage": USAGE,
            "total_cost_usd": 0.03,
            "num_turns": 1,
            "duration_ms": 900,
            "session_id": "fake-session",
        })
        return 0

    if MODE == "echo_prompt":
        # Reports how the prompt arrived rather than answering it, so a test
        # can see that a 500 KB judge prompt travelled on stdin and not in
        # argv (Linux caps a single argument at 128 KB — the harness used to
        # die there with an uncaught OSError).
        stdin_text = "" if sys.stdin.isatty() else sys.stdin.read()
        emit({"result": json.dumps({
                  "stdin_bytes": len(stdin_text.encode("utf-8")),
                  "stdin_sha256": hashlib.sha256(
                      stdin_text.encode("utf-8")).hexdigest(),
                  "argv": sys.argv[1:],
                  "argv_max_len": max([len(a) for a in sys.argv[1:]] or [0])}),
              "is_error": False, "usage": USAGE, "total_cost_usd": 0.01,
              "num_turns": 1, "duration_ms": 100, "session_id": "fake-session"})
        return 0

    if MODE == "agent_env":
        # Reply with the probe variables the harness was asked to set, so a
        # test can see the fixture's `env:` block reached the subprocess with
        # $WORKSPACE expanded — and that PATH was prepended, not replaced.
        emit({"result": json.dumps({"probe": os.environ.get("SKILLS_EVALS_PROBE"),
                                    "path": os.environ.get("PATH"),
                                    "cwd": os.getcwd()}),
              "is_error": False, "usage": USAGE, "total_cost_usd": 0.01,
              "num_turns": 1, "duration_ms": 100, "session_id": "fake-session"})
        return 0

    if MODE == "agent_and_judge":
        emit({
            "result": json.dumps({"dimensions": JUDGE_DIMENSIONS, "overall": 7.5}),
            "is_error": False,
            "usage": USAGE,
            "total_cost_usd": 0.05,
            "num_turns": 2,
            "duration_ms": 5000,
            "session_id": "fake-session",
        })
        return 0

    if MODE in ("guidance_probe", "guidance_blind", "guidance_probe_literal"):
        # EVERY magic word in context, not the first: an arm can carry more
        # than one (the control's decoy plus a contaminating ambient file),
        # and a probe that reported only the first would let the treatment
        # token hide behind the decoy — the contamination case the guard's
        # forbidden-token side exists to catch.
        found = [] if MODE == "guidance_blind" else MAGIC_RE.findall(_loaded_memory())
        if MODE == "guidance_probe_literal" and not _asked_for_every_word():
            # A SINGULAR question, answered literally. Not disobedience: it is
            # what that question asks for, and it is the answer that let a
            # contaminated control score clean.
            found = found[:1]
        emit({"result": " ".join(found) if found else "NO-MAGIC-WORD",
             "is_error": False, "usage": USAGE, "total_cost_usd": 0.01,
             "num_turns": 1, "duration_ms": 100, "session_id": "fake-session"})
        return 0

    if MODE == "canary_loader":
        emit({"result": _load_claude_md(), "is_error": False, "usage": USAGE,
             "total_cost_usd": 0.01, "num_turns": 1, "duration_ms": 100,
             "session_id": "fake-session"})
        return 0

    if MODE == "canary_blind":
        emit({"result": "NO-MAGIC-WORD", "is_error": False, "usage": USAGE,
             "total_cost_usd": 0.01, "num_turns": 1, "duration_ms": 100,
             "session_id": "fake-session"})
        return 0

    if MODE == "canary_forager":
        content = "".join(p.read_text(encoding="utf-8") for p in sorted(Path.cwd().glob("*.md")))
        emit({"result": content, "is_error": False, "usage": USAGE,
             "total_cost_usd": 0.01, "num_turns": 1, "duration_ms": 100,
             "session_id": "fake-session"})
        return 0

    # default / "agent"
    emit({
        "result": "Filtered every pull_request/push workflow on its salient paths.",
        "is_error": False,
        "usage": USAGE,
        "total_cost_usd": 0.04,
        "num_turns": 3,
        "duration_ms": 9000,
        "session_id": "fake-session",
    })
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    raise SystemExit(main())
